{
  "id": "ur_bgn_pcgn",
  "title": "Urdu — BGN/PCGN 2007 (Hunterian-based)",
  "language": "ur",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "geographic",
  "status": "current",
  "year": 2007,
  "authority": "US Board on Geographic Names / UK Permanent Committee on Geographical Names, 23rd BGN/PCGN Conference (2007); checked November 2017",
  "description": "BGN/PCGN system for Urdu geographical names in Pakistan, based on the Hunterian system of the Surveys of India and Pakistan, with diacritics that make it reversible: ṭ ḍ ṛ, ñ for nūn ghunnah, w for و, chh for چھ, e and o, ai and au, Arabic letters as in BGN/PCGN Persian (s̄ ḩ z̄ ş ẕ ţ z̧ ‘). Removing the diacritics gives Hunterian spellings.",
  "sources": [
    "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5ab4e64d40f0b67d64e21540/ROMANIZATION_OF_URDU.pdf",
    "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/romanisation-systems",
    "https://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_ur.pdf"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Note 1: a word-final zīr in the first element of a compound marks the izafat and is written -e. Note 5: z·h, s·h with a middle dot.",
    "Final silent ہ/ه after a consonant is written -ah (Gīdaṛah, Ḩaqdarah); some census examples in the table drop the h (Āgra, Kachkīna).",
    "The table does not cover the Arabic article; it is written as in BGN/PCGN Pashto and Pakistani practice: ul, assimilated before sun letters (ud Dīn, ur Raḩmān), lower case and separate, Al at the start of a name, and -ullāh joined (Raḩmatullāh).",
    "Majhūl vowels: unvocalised medial ی after a consonant is read e and و is read o; ī/ū need kasrah/ḍammah or the lexicon."
  ],
